 (319, 'Let us think and reflect – Extract 2 & II', [
  'Extract 2: (i) …it was afternoon – Mrs. Cotter stays in bed only in the morning.',
  '(ii) B. She is unable to understand why people need medical help.',
  '(iii) She says, “I’ve never been to a doctor in my life.”',
  '(iv) True',
  'II. 1. She thinks bankers lead a dull, mechanical life – travelling, reading only newspapers – and wrongly believes they “can’t think”. She judges people quickly.',
  '2. She thinks polite conversation only means greetings and filling silences. She does not know that it means choosing kind, safe topics and not criticising people.',
  '3. It shows an awkward, uncomfortable silence – everyone is shocked or offended.',
  '4. First she is confident and eager to impress; after each blunder she becomes sorry, red-faced and nervous; at the end she is so confused that she says the opposite.',
  '5. They correct her firmly but do not shout or insult her – they are dignified and well-mannered, though clearly displeased; finally they leave politely.',
  '6. One thoughtless speaker spoils the whole visit and her mother’s hopes. It shows that polite conversation is necessary to make and keep friends.',
  '7. Because people judge a family by its manners. In society, relationships depend on speaking kindly and suitably.',
  '8. She says exactly what she thinks. Examples: “bankers can’t think” (Mr. Harding is a banker); “that dog always looks very dirty” (it is their dog Towzer).',
  '9. The mother expects kind, pleasing talk; Mary does the exact opposite each time. The bigger the gap, the louder we laugh – especially at “Must you stay? Can’t you go?”',
  '10. Surprise, displeasure and embarrassment – shown by short, cold replies (“I have no children; believe me”), looks at each other, silence and an early departure.',
  '11. Innocent. She wants to please but lacks experience and tact. She is honest, talkative and careless – not wicked.']),
